/-!
# Regular-immersion geometry and intrinsic virtual dimension

Source: Stacks Project, Divisors, Tags 0E9J, 067P, 067Q and 067R; Algebra,
Tags 00NR, 061L and 00ON; Morphisms, Tags 02FX and 02G1; ILO XVI, Definitions
2.5.1--2.5.3 and XVII, Lemma 2.4.3.4
(blueprint: lem-per-regular-lci-geometric-calculus).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Immersions between regular Noetherian schemes are regular, after factoring through
an open ambient subscheme. Source: Stacks, Tag 0E9J
(blueprint: lem-per-regular-lci-geometric-calculus).

Proof (node steps 1--2): factor the immersion as closed followed by open. The local
surjection has regular source and quotient, so its kernel is generated by an initial
segment of a regular system of parameters (regular-local-quotient-kernel-parameters).
Such a segment is regular (regular-local-generators-regular-sequence). Clear denominators
on an affine neighbourhood and apply regular-sequence-principal-neighbourhood. Invert
finitely many more denominators to generate the whole ideal. Localization preserves the
successive injections and the final quotient remains nonzero at the retained source point.
Choose the resulting lengths at the source points. -/
theorem exists_regularClosedImmersion_factor_of_regular
    {Y X : Scheme.{u}} [IsNoetherian Y] [IsNoetherian X]
    (i : Y ⟶ X) [IsImmersion i] (hY : IsRegularScheme Y) (hX : IsRegularScheme X) :
    ∃ (U : X.Opens) (j : Y ⟶ U.toScheme) (c : Y → ℕ),
      j ≫ U.ι = i ∧ IsRegularClosedImmersionWithCodimension j c := by
  sorry

/-- The codimension is locally constant, is the rank of the conormal module at every
source point, and is independent of the local equations. Source: Stacks, Tags 061L, 00LN
and 063H (blueprint: lem-per-regular-lci-geometric-calculus).

Proof (node step 2): regular-sequence-conormal-free identifies the conormal on each
regular-equation chart with a free module on its equations. Localizing gives the conormal
of the stalk kernel. Reducing a free-module isomorphism modulo the maximal ideal proves
uniqueness of rank. Thus every point on the same chart has the same length, giving local
constancy and independence of equations. -/
theorem regularClosedImmersion_codimension_rank_unique
    {Y X : Scheme.{u}} [IsNoetherian Y] [IsNoetherian X]
    (i : Y ⟶ X) (c : Y → ℕ) (hc : IsRegularClosedImmersionWithCodimension i c) :
    IsLocallyConstant c ∧
      (∀ y : Y, c y = Module.finrank
        (X.presheaf.stalk (i y) ⧸ RingHom.ker (i.stalkMap y).hom)
        (RingHom.ker (i.stalkMap y).hom).Cotangent) ∧
      ∀ d : Y → ℕ, IsRegularClosedImmersionWithCodimension i d → c = d := by
  sorry

/-- Flat base change preserves regular closed immersions and their codimension function.
Source: Stacks, Tag 067P (blueprint: lem-per-regular-lci-geometric-calculus).

Proof (node step 3): tensor each multiplication injection in the regular equations with
the flat base algebra (flat-tensor-exact). The tensor-quotient-base-change isomorphism
identifies the resulting maps with multiplication on the base-changed prefix quotients.
At a source point the final quotient is nonzero; weakly-regular-sequence-localization-prime
therefore gives regularity. Spread the equations by step 2 and compute their conormal
rank with regular-sequence-conormal-free. The length is unchanged. -/
theorem regularClosedImmersion_flat_baseChange
    {Y X X' : Scheme.{u}} [IsNoetherian Y] [IsNoetherian X] [IsNoetherian X']
    (i : Y ⟶ X) (g : X' ⟶ X) [Flat g] (c : Y → ℕ)
    (hc : IsRegularClosedImmersionWithCodimension i c) :
    IsRegularClosedImmersionWithCodimension (pullback.snd i g)
      (fun y' ↦ c (pullback.fst i g y')) := by
  sorry

/-- Composition preserves regular closed immersions and adds their codimensions.
Source: Stacks, Tags 067Q and 065K (blueprint: lem-per-regular-lci-geometric-calculus).

Proof (node step 4): lift the regular equations of the smaller closed subscheme through
the quotient by the ambient equations, and concatenate the two lists. The first
multiplication tests are those of the ambient list; the remaining prefix quotients are
exactly those of the quotient list. The final quotient is the nonzero source local ring.
Spread the sequence as in step 2 and use conormal freeness to compute its summed length. -/
theorem regularClosedImmersion_comp_codimension
    {Z Y X : Scheme.{u}} [IsNoetherian Z] [IsNoetherian Y] [IsNoetherian X]
    (i : Z ⟶ Y) (j : Y ⟶ X) (c : Z → ℕ) (d : Y → ℕ)
    (hc : IsRegularClosedImmersionWithCodimension i c)
    (hd : IsRegularClosedImmersionWithCodimension j d) :
    IsRegularClosedImmersionWithCodimension (i ≫ j) (fun z ↦ c z + d (i z)) := by
  sorry

/-- A section of a separated smooth morphism of relative dimension `r` is regularly closed
of codimension `r`. Source: Stacks, Tags 067R, 06A9 and 02G1
(blueprint: lem-per-regular-lci-geometric-calculus).

Proof (node step 5): section-separated-closed-immersion makes the section closed.
Apply smooth-pair-regular-immersion to the smooth ambient and the identity of the base.
For a local splitting R → B → R, smooth-conormal-sequence-exact identifies J/J² with
Ω(B/R) tensor R. Smooth-omega-finite-locally-free gives its rank r, and
regular-sequence-conormal-free identifies this rank with the number of equations.
The argument also applies to r = 0. -/
theorem regularClosedImmersion_smooth_section
    {V S : Scheme.{u}} [IsNoetherian V] [IsNoetherian S]
    (p : V ⟶ S) (s : S ⟶ V) (r : ℕ) [IsSeparated p]
    [SmoothOfRelativeDimension r p] (hs : s ≫ p = 𝟙 S) :
    IsRegularClosedImmersionWithCodimension s (fun _ ↦ r) := by
  sorry

/-- A smooth scheme over a regular Noetherian scheme is regular. Source: Stacks, Tag 07NF
(blueprint: lem-per-regular-lci-geometric-calculus).

Proof (node step 6): on compatible affine charts apply regular-ring-smooth-ascent to the
smooth ring map and the regular base ring. These charts give regularity of the source.
In particular this applies to affine space, whose projection is smooth by
sbc-lem-affine-space-smooth. -/
theorem isRegularScheme_of_smooth_over_regular
    {Y X : Scheme.{u}} [IsNoetherian Y] [IsNoetherian X]
    (f : Y ⟶ X) [Smooth f] (hX : IsRegularScheme X) : IsRegularScheme Y := by
  sorry

/-- A finite-type map of regular affine Noetherian schemes has an affine-space closed
presentation; every such closed presentation is regular. Source: Stacks, Tags 01T2 and 00NR
(blueprint: lem-per-regular-lci-geometric-calculus).

Proof (node step 7): lft-affine-pairs makes the target ring a finitely generated algebra
of the base ring. Choose finite generators and evaluate the polynomial algebra onto it.
The surjection gives a closed immersion into affine space over the base. Affine space is
regular by smooth ascent, so steps 1--2 make this and every other closed presentation
regular. The empty set of polynomial variables is allowed. -/
theorem regular_affine_finiteType_closed_presentation
    {Y X : Scheme.{u}} [IsNoetherian Y] [IsNoetherian X] [IsAffine Y] [IsAffine X]
    (f : Y ⟶ X) [LocallyOfFiniteType f] [QuasiCompact f]
    (hY : IsRegularScheme Y) (hX : IsRegularScheme X) :
    (∃ (r : ℕ) (i : Y ⟶ AffineSpace (ULift.{u} (Fin r)) X),
      i ≫ (AffineSpace (ULift.{u} (Fin r)) X ↘ X) = f ∧ IsClosedImmersion i) ∧
    ∀ (r : ℕ) (i : Y ⟶ AffineSpace (ULift.{u} (Fin r)) X),
      i ≫ (AffineSpace (ULift.{u} (Fin r)) X ↘ X) = f → IsClosedImmersion i →
        ∃ c : Y → ℕ, IsRegularClosedImmersionWithCodimension i c := by
  sorry

/-- Intrinsic virtual dimension, with relative dimensions allowed to vary on components.
The natural numbers `dx`, `dy`, and `t` record the finite local dimensions and residue
transcendence degree, so the equality is an equality of integers without truncated
subtraction. Source: Stacks, Tags 00ON, 02FX and 02G1; ILO XVI 2.5.1--2.5.3
(blueprint: lem-per-regular-lci-geometric-calculus).

Proof (node steps 8--9): smooth-criterion gives flatness of the stalk map. Apply
flat-height-formula and identify its closed-fibre local ring by fibre-stalk-quotient.
The field dimension formula gives dim O(P,z) + trdeg κ(z)/κ(x) = dim O(X,x) + r(z).
Localize the regular equations using weakly-regular-sequence-localization-prime and
apply regular-sequence-dimension-formula to obtain dim O(Y,y) + c(y) = dim O(P,i(y)).
Noetherian-local-finite-krull-dimension makes these finite ordinary dimensions. A quotient
local map identifies the source and image residue fields. Substitute and rearrange in ℤ.
Local constancy follows from that of smooth fibre dimension and the conormal rank. -/
theorem regular_lci_virtual_dimension_formula
    {Y X P : Scheme.{u}} [IsNoetherian Y] [IsNoetherian X] [IsNoetherian P]
    [IsAffine Y] [IsAffine X] (f : Y ⟶ X) [LocallyOfFiniteType f] [QuasiCompact f]
    (hY : IsRegularScheme Y) (hX : IsRegularScheme X)
    (i : Y ⟶ P) (p : P ⟶ X) (hi : i ≫ p = f) [Smooth p]
    (c : Y → ℕ) (hc : IsRegularClosedImmersionWithCodimension i c)
    (r : P → ℕ) (hr : ∀ z : P, topologicalKrullDimAt (p.asFiber z) =
      (r z : WithBot ℕ∞)) :
    IsLocallyConstant (fun y : Y ↦ (r (i y) : ℤ) - (c y : ℤ)) ∧
      ∀ y : Y, ∃ dx dy t : ℕ,
        ringKrullDim (X.presheaf.stalk (f y)) = (dx : WithBot ℕ∞) ∧
        ringKrullDim (Y.presheaf.stalk y) = (dy : WithBot ℕ∞) ∧
        @Algebra.trdeg (X.residueField (f y)) (Y.residueField y) _ _
          (f.residueFieldMap y).hom.toAlgebra = (t : Cardinal.{u}) ∧
        (r (i y) : ℤ) - (c y : ℤ) = (dy : ℤ) - (dx : ℤ) + (t : ℤ) := by
  sorry

/-- The virtual dimension is independent of the regular-immersion/smooth factorization.
Source: ILO XVI 2.5.1--2.5.3 (blueprint: lem-per-regular-lci-geometric-calculus).

Proof (node step 9): apply the intrinsic dimension formula to both factorizations at the
same point. Their right hand sides are the same local ring dimensions and the same
residue-field transcendence degree. Finite-cardinal and finite-dimension coercions are
injective, so the integers on the left agree. -/
theorem regular_lci_virtual_dimension_independent
    {Y X P Q : Scheme.{u}} [IsNoetherian Y] [IsNoetherian X]
    [IsNoetherian P] [IsNoetherian Q] [IsAffine Y] [IsAffine X]
    (f : Y ⟶ X) [LocallyOfFiniteType f] [QuasiCompact f]
    (hY : IsRegularScheme Y) (hX : IsRegularScheme X)
    (i : Y ⟶ P) (p : P ⟶ X) (j : Y ⟶ Q) (q : Q ⟶ X)
    (hi : i ≫ p = f) (hj : j ≫ q = f) [Smooth p] [Smooth q]
    (c d : Y → ℕ) (hc : IsRegularClosedImmersionWithCodimension i c)
    (hd : IsRegularClosedImmersionWithCodimension j d)
    (r : P → ℕ) (s : Q → ℕ)
    (hr : ∀ z : P, topologicalKrullDimAt (p.asFiber z) = (r z : WithBot ℕ∞))
    (hs : ∀ z : Q, topologicalKrullDimAt (q.asFiber z) = (s z : WithBot ℕ∞)) :
    ∀ y : Y, (r (i y) : ℤ) - (c y : ℤ) = (s (j y) : ℤ) - (d y : ℤ) := by
  sorry

/-- Finite-type maps with finite fibres have finite residue extensions of transcendence
degree zero; in particular this holds for quasi-finite maps in the recorded Stacks sense.
Source: Stacks, Tags 01TG, 01TH and 0ALW; ILO XVII 2.4.3.4
(blueprint: lem-per-regular-lci-geometric-calculus).

Proof (node step 10): each finite-type affine fibre chart has finite spectrum, so
finite-spectrum-maximal-primes says all its primes are maximal. Every fibre point is
therefore closed and, in a finite space, isolated. Apply isolated-fibre-point-residue-finite.
Finite-dimensional field extensions are algebraic since the powers of any element are
linearly dependent, hence their transcendence degree is zero. For a quasi-finite map,
isolation is already part of the recorded definition. Step 11: all pointwise conclusions
are vacuous for empty sources, and the empty regular sequence handles codimension zero. -/
theorem finite_residue_extension_trdeg_zero_of_quasiFinite
    {Y X : Scheme.{u}} [IsNoetherian Y] [IsNoetherian X]
    (f : Y ⟶ X)
    (hf : (LocallyOfFiniteType f ∧ QuasiCompact f ∧ f.HasFiniteFibres) ∨ f.IsQuasiFinite) :
    ∀ y : Y,
      @FiniteDimensional (X.residueField (f y)) (Y.residueField y) _ _
        (f.residueFieldMap y).hom.toAlgebra.toModule ∧
      @Algebra.trdeg (X.residueField (f y)) (Y.residueField y) _ _
        (f.residueFieldMap y).hom.toAlgebra = 0 := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
